WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:19.065
Very early this morning, I was sent to perform a procedure called bloodletting on a young girl suffering from cholera and I could not do it.

2
00:00:19.066 --> 00:00:22.656
Perhaps it would be more truthful to say I refused to do it.

3
00:00:22.657 --> 00:00:32.002
I have come to believe that bloodletting, the taking of blood from a patient, is not only without benefit to a person with cholera, but is indeed harmful.

4
00:00:32.003 --> 00:00:39.621
My name is Mathew Brogan and I am a doctor in the Indian Medical Service, which serves the British Empire in India.

5
00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:43.967
The year is 1875 and I am stationed in Calcutta.

6
00:00:43.968 --> 00:00:51.400
Before I came to India, I graduated from the medical school at the University of Edinburgh in Scotland after two years of study.

7
00:00:51.401 --> 00:01:00.696
My education included physiology, anatomy, surgery, pharmacy, pathology, and the diseases of men, women, and children.

8
00:01:00.697 --> 00:01:07.119
But nothing I was taught prepared me for the cholera epidemics that are so prevalent in Europe and Asia these days.

9
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:15.547
Hundreds of thousands of people have died from this disease, which causes abdominal distress and results in the loss of large amounts of body fluids.

10
00:01:15.548 --> 00:01:22.454
There is no cure for cholera as of yet; many remedies have been tried, but nothing works, least of all bloodletting.

11
00:01:22.455 --> 00:01:28.906
Yesterday my colleague Doctor McGregor and I were called to a house where the whole family was sick with the disease.

12
00:01:28.907 --> 00:01:34.584
The air was filled with the stench of vomit and feces, and the house was full of bodies.

13
00:01:34.585 --> 00:01:37.559
It was hard to tell who was alive and who was dead.

14
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:44.214
According to the neighbors, the family had just returned from a pilgrimage to the Temple of Jagannatha in the town of Puri.

15
00:01:44.215 --> 00:01:51.384
This fact reminded me that large gatherings of people tend to trigger cholera outbreaks, but the reason why is unclear.

16
00:01:51.385 --> 00:01:55.532
We walked past an older man lying on a cot outside the front door.

17
00:01:55.533 --> 00:02:01.380
It was obvious that nothing could be done for him; his eyes were deep in their sockets and his skin was blue.

18
00:02:01.381 --> 00:02:06.325
The sick man resembled a raisin—a dried fruit that has lost all its moisture.

19
00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:12.082
I kneeled next to two children inside the house who were also near death and examined them.

20
00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:17.838
A woman, probably their mother, was nearby; she was vomiting and had severe diarrhea.

21
00:02:17.839 --> 00:02:23.731
McGregor instructed me to bleed her, so I used my spring lancet, a very efficient tool.

22
00:02:23.732 --> 00:02:26.979
But I had a difficult time piercing her shriveled skin.

23
00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.180
The blood, when it finally emerged, was dark, thick, and gummy.

24
00:02:31.181 --> 00:02:34.402
How could such thick blood flow through the heart?

25
00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:42.398
The woman died within the hour, and I could only ask myself, why did we try to take more fluid from a body that was already dry?

26
00:02:42.399 --> 00:02:46.336
As I walked home with Dr. McGregor, I was troubled and said to him,

27
00:02:46.337 --> 00:02:55.737
"At Edinburgh, my professor, Dr. Joseph Lister, spoke of a theory proposing that a bacterium, an invisible organism, causes cholera.

28
00:02:55.738 --> 00:03:02.176
Another prominent doctor supports that claim too and believes the germ is carried by dirty drinking water."

29
00:03:02.177 --> 00:03:06.461
McGregor stopped and faced me. He pushed his finger into my chest.

30
00:03:06.462 --> 00:03:10.848
"I've heard of those tiny germs, and I don't believe in them," he said.

31
00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:14.642
"Nobody believes in them! It's just bad air, Brogan.

32
00:03:14.643 --> 00:03:19.354
Besides cholera usually affects only the ignorant and the poor."

33
00:03:19.355 --> 00:03:24.828
I wondered to myself how air breathed by the poor would be different than air breathed by the wealthy.

34
00:03:24.829 --> 00:03:28.293
It is true that the poor get the disease more than the rich,

35
00:03:28.294 --> 00:03:34.203
but the reason for that could be the conditions in which they live—the crowding, the lack of sanitation.

36
00:03:34.204 --> 00:03:41.303
Lister's germs thrive in filth, so perhaps the cholera germ, whatever it is, thrives in filth as well.

37
00:03:41.304 --> 00:03:44.722
"That woman had no fluids left," I said to McGregor.

38
00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:49.225
"Her blood barely flowed. Perhaps we should not have taken her blood."

39
00:03:49.226 --> 00:03:56.924
But McGregor insisted that he had been treating cholera since 1853, and that bloodletting was an appropriate treatment for the disease.

40
00:03:56.925 --> 00:04:03.663
Despite McGregor's opinion, when I was called to that young girl's side this morning, I decided to try something different.

41
00:04:03.664 --> 00:04:08.550
Her face looked haggard, and she complained of stomach cramps and occasional diarrhea.

42
00:04:08.551 --> 00:04:13.852
When I informed her it was the first stages of cholera, she seemed resigned to her death.

43
00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:20.853
We often use a mustard plaster to treat cholera because it is believed that the plaster eases a patient's pain and cramping.

44
00:04:20.854 --> 00:04:29.706
A mustard plaster is made with a paste of dry mustard, a bit of flour, and an egg, which is then spread on a cloth and placed on the abdomen or back.

45
00:04:29.707 --> 00:04:35.973
Once placed on the patient's skin, the mustard plaster becomes hot and draws the body's blood to that area.

46
00:04:35.974 --> 00:04:44.336
But this time I decided against that remedy and settled on opium, a medicine I know to be effective in relieving diarrhea and cramps.

47
00:04:44.337 --> 00:04:48.539
I also offered her sips of cool ginger tea that I brought with me.

48
00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:57.123
Just in case the new theories are right and the disease is indeed carried in contaminated water, I asked her mother to boil any water they drink.

49
00:04:57.124 --> 00:05:01.004
I checked on her at noon, in between seeing other patients.

50
00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:06.484
Even though she had started vomiting, she was not becoming shriveled like so many other victims.

51
00:05:06.485 --> 00:05:12.715
I instructed her mother to keep wet compresses on her forehead (using clean, cooled water only)

52
00:05:12.716 --> 00:05:17.774
and to continue giving her daughter as many sips of ginger tea as she would tolerate.

53
00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:21.527
"She needs to replace the fluids she's lost," I admonished.

54
00:05:21.528 --> 00:05:24.374
In the evening I checked on my patient again.

55
00:05:24.375 --> 00:05:29.021
She was feverish, and although her mother was concerned, I was gratified.

56
00:05:29.022 --> 00:05:34.608
Fever is a symptom that tells the doctor the body is fighting back against an infectious disease.

57
00:05:34.609 --> 00:05:38.861
After convincing them both that she would recover, I headed home.

58
00:05:38.862 --> 00:05:43.712
One patient is not sufficient proof, but I am glad I did not bleed this patient.

59
00:05:43.713 --> 00:05:50.244
I will continue to experiment with other kinds of treatments, and someday I hope to understand what causes cholera.

60
00:05:50.245 --> 00:05:58.799
These are exciting times to be a doctor, and I look forward to finding a cure for this devastating disease.
